{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 40,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On November 22, 2014"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2360,
    "dDurationMs": 2920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a burst of x-rays was detected by ASASSN"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5280,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that's the All Sky Automated Survey for Super Novae"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8500,
    "dDurationMs": 2380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but this was no supernova"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10880,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the signal came  from the center of a galaxy around 290 million light-years away"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16520,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and what we now belive happened was a star came too close to a supermassive black hole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21300,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with a mass millions of times that of our Sun and it was eaten"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25460,
    "dDurationMs": 2500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The black hole fed on the star"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27960,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and yes, this is the actual termenology astrophysicists use to describe it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32520,
    "dDurationMs": 1880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Events like these are thought to be rare"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34400,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "occurring maybe once every 10,000 to 100,000 years in a galaxy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38560,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they're called Tidal Disruption Events, or Tidal Disruption Flares"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42460,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as the star approached the side closest to the black hole experienced a much gravitational pull than the other side,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49460,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ripping the star to shreds"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51620,
    "dDurationMs": 3620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter spiraling into the black hole formed an accretion disk"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55240,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an annular ring of gas and dust"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57520,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that's accelerating and heating up emitting visible light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60460,
    "dDurationMs": 2920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "UV and X-rays observable from Earth"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64660,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now what's remarkable about this event is that it transformed a dormant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68080,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or quiescent black hole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69580,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one that wasn't really feeding into one that we can observe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72380,
    "dDurationMs": 2500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thanks to the matter falling in from that star"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74880,
    "dDurationMs": 2420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and is what it looked like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78120,
    "dDurationMs": 1420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okay if you're disappointed,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79540,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "check out these artists renditions of the same event"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87440,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but if you're cynical you might say"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88960,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"Well how do we know that's what really happened?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91660,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what if the scientists are just making this up to get more grant funding"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94360,
    "dDurationMs": 2420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or to inspire people to go into science\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97140,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "well I'll explain how we know this is actually what went down"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100500,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but first things got wierder"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103020,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scientists trained three X-ray telescopes to observe this part of the sky for years after the event"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108960,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and what they found was a strong and regular pulse of X-rays"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113000,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "brightening and dimming every 131 seconds"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116100,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it shows up in the data from all three telescopes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118740,
    "dDurationMs": 2780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they observed periodically over 450 days"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121520,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the pulse maintained this rhythm and didn't get weaker"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124580,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in fact as time went on the relative strength of the pulse got stronger"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128120,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "modulating the X-ray signal by around 40%"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131680,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and what was causing these periodic flashes of X-rays"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134980,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and what could it tell us about the black hole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138100,
    "dDurationMs": 1220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "well let's back up"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139320,
    "dDurationMs": 3580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because black holes are some of the simplest objects in the Universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142900,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by that I just mean that they are characterized by only two attributes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146920,
    "dDurationMs": 1940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mass and Spin"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148860,
    "dDurationMs": 1660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okay, there's also charge"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150520,
    "dDurationMs": 3340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but since black holes should essentially be neutral mass and spin"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153860,
    "dDurationMs": 1340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are the two that count"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155700,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass is relatively easy to determine"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158640,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "far away from a black hole, you can even use Newtonian physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162180,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By measuring the gravitational effects of the black hole on other bodies，"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165780,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you can estimate the mass of of the black hole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168180,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this has been done and black holes been found with masses ranging from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171700,
    "dDurationMs": 2620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just few times our Sun, stellar-mass black holes,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174320,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up to billions of solar masses -- supermassive black holes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178240,
    "dDurationMs": 6380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's generally accepted that there is a supermassive black hole at the centers of most galaxies, including our own."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184940,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what about spin?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186560,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "since black holes form from collapsing stars and all known starts rotate,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191440,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all black holes should also be rotating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194160,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mean, what are the chances that a bunch of matter just collapses into a point perfectly with no rotation?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199580,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's just not going to happen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201980,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And then additional matter falling into the black hole contributes its angular momentum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206560,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So like a figure skater pulling their arms into a... point object,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210960,
    "dDurationMs": 3020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can imagine black holes get spinning pretty fast."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213980,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But spin is harder to measure because unlike mass, it only affects objects relatively close to the black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220000,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there is a way to do it. Actually, 3 ways."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223440,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To understand all of them, you have to understand isco."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227260,
    "dDurationMs": 2680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In Newtonian physics, around a compact mass,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229940,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you can place an object in a circular object at any radius and it will be stable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234240,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It doesn't matter how close you get."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236320,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is not the case according to general relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239140,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here, there is an innermost stable circular orbit,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243120,
    "dDurationMs": 2780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With a radius know as r-isco."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246460,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Closer than this, and no orbits are stable: they all fall into the black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251240,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So when you're looking at a black hole that is feeding, the innermost edge of the accretion disk is at r-isco."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257840,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What's useful for our purposes is that r-isco depends on the spin of the black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262320,
    "dDurationMs": 3820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The faster it's spinning, the smaller r-isco becomes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266140,
    "dDurationMs": 3700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Assuming it's spinning in the same direction as the matter in the accretion disk."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270300,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The rotation enables particles to orbit closer to the black hole,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273900,
    "dDurationMs": 2620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than they'd be able to for a non-spinning black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276520,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So you can kind of think of it as though the spin is supporting the particles against the relentless pull of gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283060,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now spin is normally discussed in terms of a dimensionless parameter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286420,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that ranges from 0, no spin, to 1, maximum spin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290380,
    "dDurationMs": 6140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Though I guess you could also have spins down to -1 if the black hole is spinning in the opposite direction from the accretion disk."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296520,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, as spin increases, r-isco decreases."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300240,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by a factor of 6, shrinking down to the size of the event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304540,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this sets what many scientists think is the maximum spin a black hole can have."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308740,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because if the minimum stable orbit were the size of the event horizon,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312860,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then light could escape from the black hole, allowing us to see into the singularity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317440,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is called a naked singularity, and it makes a lot of scientists uncomfortable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321940,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As yet, there isn't a strong theoretical reason why a black hole can't exceed this maximum spin,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 326940,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's just that we haven't seen one, and the thought of an exposed singularity just kind of... feels wrong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333380,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Most suspect the maximum real-world spin parameter is around 0.998."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338660,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how can you use r-isco to measure the spin of a black hole?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342260,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well first, let's think of how we measure the size of anything far away from us in deep space, like the radius of a star"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348580,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Most stars are so far away that they're simply point objects in our telescopes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352960,
    "dDurationMs": 1700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how can you figure out their radii?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355020,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, first, look at the spectrum of their light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357180,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By seeing how red-shifted absorption lines are, you can determine how far away the star is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362020,
    "dDurationMs": 2680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The spectrum also tells you the temperature of the star --"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364700,
    "dDurationMs": 2260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because it should approximate a black-body curve."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367540,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And now the power radiated per unit area is strongly dependent on its temperature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372860,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if you know how bright the star appears from Earth,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375700,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how far away it is, and how much power its radiating per unit area,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379700,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, then you could work out its area and hence its radius."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383820,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can actually so something very similar for a black hole's accretion disk."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387460,
    "dDurationMs": 2620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just instead of estimating the radius of a glowing sphere,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390080,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you're estimating the radius of the dark circle, r-isco, in the middle of the glowing accretion disk."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395240,
    "dDurationMs": 2920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then you can use r-isco to find the spin parameter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398160,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This has been done for a number of black holes, revealing spin parameters from around 0.1 up to close to the maximum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404840,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this method only works if the radiation from the black hole is dominated by black-body radiation from the accretion disk."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411400,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which, often, it's not."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413430,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another approach involves looking at x-rays emitted by iron around a black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418050,
    "dDurationMs": 3230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some black holes show a distinct iron emission line."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421280,
    "dDurationMs": 2370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But instead of the single frequency you'd expect,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423650,
    "dDurationMs": 5630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the line is broadened by factors like the Doppler shift due to the high velocity of the iron in the accretion disk."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429280,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and gravitational redshift, due to the extreme gravitational fields close to the black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434310,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By looking at the low-energy limit of the iron emission line,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437310,
    "dDurationMs": 2510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you can determine how close the black hole it was emitted."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439820,
    "dDurationMs": 1550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And hence, r-isco."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441940,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what if there is no bright iron emission line?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445020,
    "dDurationMs": 2150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, luckily, there is a third way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 447170,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that, is to look for periodic oscillations in the data."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450710,
    "dDurationMs": 3890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like the repeated x-rays observed every 131 seconds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454600,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The thinking is, these cycles must be caused by clumps of matter orbiting the black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459770,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And at frequencies that high, they must be orbiting very close in, probably near r-isco."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464880,
    "dDurationMs": 2830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even that close, they'd be going half the speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468020,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what kind of clumps or objects would these be?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470820,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well the study's authors argue that the best candidate involves an unlikely scenario:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475570,
    "dDurationMs": 6340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Years before the tidal disruption event, they propose that there was a white dwarf start in orbit around this black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481910,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, it might be stable, orbiting in this way, for perhaps one or two hundred years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486280,
    "dDurationMs": 2740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By itself it wouldn't be visible from Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489020,
    "dDurationMs": 4830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But then the other star wondered by, and was ripped apart in the tidal disruption event."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493850,
    "dDurationMs": 3460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Its mass fell in towards the black hole forming an accretion disk."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497310,
    "dDurationMs": 4710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With the addition of this stellar debris, the white dwarf was cloaked in glowing matter,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502020,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "creating an x-ray hotspot orbiting the black hole, and its period would directly relate to the spin of the black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508680,
    "dDurationMs": 7340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this case, the measured spin parameter turned out to be at least 0.7 and possibly as high as the theoretical maximum of 0.998,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516020,
    "dDurationMs": 3860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "meaning objects in the accretion disk were going at least half the speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520310,
    "dDurationMs": 4710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the first measurement of spin made possible by a tidal disruption event."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525020,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The implication is that this could provide a method for determining the spin of black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529480,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Particularly ones that have been dormant, which is about 95% of supermassive black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535020,
    "dDurationMs": 3430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If they shred a star, we get insight into their spin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538940,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now why is this important? Well, because it helps us understand the origins of black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544910,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If supermassive black holes grow in size mainly by feeding on a steady stream of matter from within their own galaxy,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551710,
    "dDurationMs": 5890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You'd expect their spins to be very large, because the angular momentum of that matter would be more or less aligned."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 557600,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so it would add up over time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559600,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if instead, supermassive black holes grow predominantly by merging with other black holes,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564970,
    "dDurationMs": 6740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You might expect their spins to be lower, because the spins of two black holes are likely to be randomly oriented rather than aligned."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 572080,
    "dDurationMs": 6340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As we are able to measure the spins of more black holes in different ways, farther out and therefore further back in time,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578400,
    "dDurationMs": 2600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We should be able to better understand their growth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581000,
    "dDurationMs": 3820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And since supermassive black holes lie at the center of most galaxies,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 584820,
    "dDurationMs": 7260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They also lie at the center of an understanding of how those galaxies have formed and evolved over billions of years."
    } ]
  } ]
}
